# React testing

Test layout, runner, and assertion conventions for React component files
(`*.tsx`/`*.jsx`), narrowing `ts-js-node`'s generic testing rule.

## Layout and runner

- Use the project's already-configured runner (Vitest or Jest) and React
  Testing Library (`@testing-library/react` + `@testing-library/user-event`)
  — do not introduce Enzyme or a shallow-render helper into a project that
  does not already use one.
- Co-locate a component's test next to it (`UserCard.test.tsx`) when the
  project already follows that layout, or under a mirrored `__tests__`
  tree when that is the existing convention; match, do not introduce.

## Queries and interaction

- Query by role/label/text the way a user or assistive technology would
  (`getByRole`, `getByLabelText`, `getByText`) — reach for `data-testid`
  only when no accessible query exists, and treat that as a sign the
  markup itself may be missing an accessible name.
- Never assert on a component's internal state, instance fields, or
  implementation details (no reaching into a ref's internals, no snapshot
  of a component's private props) — assert on rendered output and
  behavior only.
- Drive interaction through `@testing-library/user-event`
  (`userEvent.click`/`.type`/`.tab`), not `fireEvent`, unless the project
  is pinned to an older `user-event` major that cannot express the
  interaction — `user-event` fires the fuller sequence of browser events a
  real interaction would.

## Async and network

- Wait for async UI with `findBy*` queries or `waitFor`, never a manual
  `setTimeout`/`sleep` — an arbitrary delay is both slow and flaky under
  load.
- Mock network calls at the request boundary (an MSW handler, or the
  project's existing equivalent) rather than mocking the component's own
  data-fetching function — a boundary mock still exercises the real
  fetch/parse/error-handling code path.
- Wrap a state update triggered outside React Testing Library's own query
  helpers (e.g. a manually invoked callback) in `act` only when RTL's
  built-in wrapping does not already cover it; an `act` warning is a
  signal to await the right query, not to silence the console.

## Coverage expectations

- Cover the rendered states a component can be in: initial/loading,
  success, error, and empty, plus any conditional branch a prop or piece
  of state gates.
- For a custom hook with logic worth testing on its own, use
  `renderHook` from `@testing-library/react` rather than mounting a
  throwaway component to exercise it.
